+# JSRF v23: repeated CMPSD flag-tracking root cause
+
+## Runtime result
+
+v23 keeps the AC'97/APU path active and reaches the DirectSound setup path.
+`sub_001680D0` returns success.  The next checked startup call,
+`sub_00116EC0`, still returns `0x800401F1`.
+
+The new vtable traces make the bad dispatch visible:
+
+1. `sub_00116EC0` calls `0x00168310` with IID pointer `0x001E391C` and root
+   object `0x0107FE30`.
+2. `0x00168310` returns success.
+3. The interface pointer subsequently used by the caller is still
+   `0x0107FE30`, whose vtable is `0x001E3A70`.
+4. Slot `+0x0C` therefore dispatches to root method `0x00168480`.
+5. That method sees the already-created internal device at object `+0x14` and
+   returns `0x800401F1`.
+
+Retail `0x00116EC0` expects QueryInterface(IID `0x001E391C`) to return the
+secondary interface.  On that interface, slot `+0x0C` is `0x00168400`, not
+`0x00168480`.
+
+## Retail QueryInterface proof
+
+Exact retail code at `0x00168310` compares the requested IID against several
+16-byte GUIDs.  Its first structural comparison is:
+
+```asm
+00168321 xor  edx, edx
+00168323 mov  ecx, 4
+00168328 mov  edi, 001E4C94
+0016832D mov  esi, eax
+0016832F repz cmpsd
+00168331 sete dl
+00168334 test dl, dl
+00168336 jne  001683E2
+```
+
+The requested IID for the failing call is `0x001E391C`.  Later in the same
+function retail has a direct/same-GUID path for that IID:
+
+```asm
+00168362 cmp  eax, 001E391C
+00168367 je   00168380
+...
+00168380 mov  eax, [esp+0C]
+0016838C add  eax, 4
+0016838F mov  [ecx], eax
+```
+
+So the correct returned interface is `this + 4`, i.e. `0x0107FE34` for the
+observed object.  Its vtable at object `+4` is `0x001E3A5C`, whose `+0x0C` slot
+is the separately proven retail method at `0x00168400`.
+
+The observed root pointer `0x0107FE30` is exactly the return path at
+`0x001683E2`, which is used for the root/identity GUIDs.
+
+## xboxrecomp semantic mismatch
+
+Pinned xboxrecomp already has executable lifts for repeated word/dword string
+comparisons.  Its CMPSW/CMPSD lift performs the comparisons, advances ESI/EDI,
+decrements ECX, and writes the final equality state to `_flags`.
+
+The block-level flag tracker is narrower.  In the pinned revision it recognizes
+only `cmpsb`/`scasb` spellings in a `rep*` instruction as the new flag producer.
+It does not recognize `cmpsw`, `cmpsd`, `scasw`, or `scasd`.
+
+That matters here because `sete dl` is not emitted in isolation when the block
+tracker thinks it knows the last EFLAGS producer.  Since `repz cmpsd` is
+incorrectly treated as flag-preserving, the tracker walks past it and retains
+the preceding `xor edx, edx` as the producer.  XOR-self sets ZF, so `sete dl`
+becomes true regardless of the GUID data actually compared.  JSRF therefore
+takes the first root-interface match path and returns `this` instead of
+`this + 4`.
+
+This matches every observed runtime fact without changing or bypassing guest
+DirectSound behavior.
+
+## v24 correction
+
+v24 changes only xboxrecomp's repeated-string **flag tracking** in the pinned
+toolkit.  The tracker now recognizes the same six comparison widths already
+implemented by the instruction lifter:
+
+- `cmpsb`, `cmpsw`, `cmpsd`
+- `scasb`, `scasw`, `scasd`
+
+The comparison loops themselves are unchanged.  No HRESULT is forced, no IID is
+special-cased in host code, and no JSRF function is replaced.
+
+The expected consequence for this exact retail sequence is that `sete dl`
+consumes the `_flags` value produced by `repz cmpsd`, allowing the real GUID
+comparison to choose the secondary `this + 4` interface naturally.
